## Running it

```sh
npm install
npm run dev        # http://localhost:5173
```

`?seed=abc&weapon=maul` replays a run exactly — the seed fixes the board and every roll in
it, the weapon fixes the one decision made before the run starts. Both are written into the
URL as soon as they are known, so any run can be replayed from the address bar.

```sh
npm test           # 407 tests
npm run typecheck
npm run build
npm run balance    # plays 12 seeds under 4 policies, prints win rates
```

## Controls

| | |
|---|---|
| Move | arrows, numpad, or vi keys (`hjkl` + `yubn`) — eight directions |
| Shove | walk into an enemy: it goes one tile down the lane you were walking |
| Wait | `.` or numpad `5` — fires your weapons, advances no step counter |
| Blink | **Shift** + a direction: 4 tiles, 8-turn cooldown |
| Level-up cards | `1`–`4` choose · `R` reroll · `Space` skip for a small heal |
| New run | `Space` on the results screen |
| Debug overlay | `F1` |

A run opens by choosing one weapon from a pool, and nothing else. `?seed=abc&weapon=maul`
replays that run exactly and skips the choice.

With the overlay open: `E` spawn an enemy · `R` cycle its kind · click a tile to spawn
there or click an actor to apply a status · `S` cycle status · `A` apply it to everyone ·
`T` jump 50 turns · `M` jump to any turn · `V` force a level-up · `G` god mode ·
`P` single-step · `,` step once · `C` clear enemies. (Debug keys avoid `hjkl`/`yubn` and
`.` so the overlay stays usable while you play.)

The overlay also opens a damage log in the top-left: what dealt how much over the last
few turns, summed per source per turn, with damage you took picked out in red.

## Layout

```
src/
  core/     grid, seeded RNG, arena generation, energy scheduler, flow field
  data/     all content as typed modules — spells and weapons, passives, enemies, timeline
  game/     the model: World, triggers, casting, shapes, ground, cards, spawner
  render/   canvas renderer, animation queue, the juice vocabulary, HUD and screens
  debug/    the F1 overlay other systems register readouts and hotkeys into
  sim/      headless bot and the balance harness behind `npm run balance`
```

Two rules shape the whole thing:

- **All content is data.** The caster branches on shape and targeting, never on a spell's
  name; a ninth spell is a ninth record in `data/spells.ts`. Weapons are records in that
  same file — melee is a bolt at range 1 — so they needed no engine of their own.
- **The model never waits on the screen.** A turn resolves the instant input arrives and
  emits events; the visual layer replays them, compressing when it falls behind and
  snapping when it falls far behind. Input is never gated on an animation.
